.TH "ODIR-RESOLVE" "7" "" "odir 25.1.2+onesimus.0.2.0" "Onesimus Director Commands"
.SH NAME
odir\-resolve \- resolve: Resolve a hostname
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
resolve [client=<client>] [fd=<fd>] [storage=<storage>] [sd=<sd>] [<hostname> ...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Shows the IP addresses a host name resolves to. Without client= or storage= the Director resolves the name itself; with client= (or fd=) the File Daemon resolves it, with storage= the Storage Daemon, and the Director prints their answer. This helps to locate name resolution problems on either side of a connection. Several host names may be given; nothing is changed.
.PP
When the Director resolves itself and a name fails, the command stops there and later names are not tried. Without any argument there is no output.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.SS "resolve"
.TP
.B "client=<client>"
Resolve on this client (name; values from .clients)
.TP
.B "fd=<fd>"
Client name (as client=) (name; values from .clients)
.TP
.B "storage=<storage>"
Resolve on this storage (name; values from .storages)
.TP
.B "sd=<sd>"
Storage name (as storage=) (name; values from .storages)
.TP
.B "<hostname> ..."
Host name to resolve (positional value; may be repeated)
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B "resolve backup.example.com"
addresses as seen by the Director
.TP
.B "resolve client=bareos\-fd backup.example.com"
how the File Daemon resolves the Director host
.SH ORIGIN
Bareos console command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR odir\-commands (7),
.BR oconsole (1)
